{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 21, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a prominent Crusader nobleman and ruler who played a significant role during the late 12th and early 13th centuries. Born likely in the year 1140 Raymond was the second son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem his mother being the sister of Baldwin III King of Jerusalem. He received his education in Europe where he was tutored by famous scholars such as John of Salisbury. Raymond succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1187 after the Battle of Hattin which resulted in the loss of much of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the capture of his father.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Role in the Third Crusade**\nRaymond participated in the Third Crusade alongside Richard the Lionheart King of England and Philip II Augustus King of France. His experience and knowledge of the Levant made him a valuable asset to the crusading armies. When Richard and Philip fell out Raymond intervened to mediate between them. His diplomacy helped prevent a schism among Christian forces which was crucial for their efforts. Though Richard eventually captured Acre in 1191 Raymond preferred to negotiate with Saladin rather than engage in further military campaigns. He played a key role in securing a truce with Saladin that allowed Christian pilgrims safe access to Jerusalem.\n\nAfter his father’s death Raymond faced significant challenges including the rise of Islamic forces and internal political conflicts within the Crusader states. He restored his realm’s defenses and worked to strengthen Tripoli’s position against external threats. Raymond also became involved in the politics of the neighboring Kingdom of Jerusalem where he frequently acted as a mediator between the king and other nobles. His political acumen earned him the respect of his contemporaries and he was often called upon to resolve disputes.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Family and Marriage**\nRaymond married Eschiva of Bures who was a member of the House of Montbéliard. Eschiva was the daughter of Hugo II Count of Bures and was previously married to William Longespée who was an illegitimate son of Henry II of England. Through his marriage Raymond strengthened his alliances with other European noble houses. Eschiva brought significant wealth and connections to the union which helped Raymond consolidate his power. The couple had several children including Bohemond who would later become the Count of Tripoli and succeed Raymond.\n\nRaymond’s reign saw the continuation of the Crusader states’ struggles against Muslim forces under the Ayyubids. His efforts focused on diplomacy and fortifications rather than large-scale military engagements. He participated in the Siege of Damietta during the Fifth Crusade in 1218 though his role was limited due to his age. Raymond died in 1275 at the age of approximately 95 making him one of the longest-lived Crusader nobles. His legacy endured through his son Bohemond who carried on his policies and further secured the independence of the County of Tripoli.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n**Legacy and Death**\nRaymond III passed away in 1275 following a long and eventful life. His reign marked a period of relative stability for the County of Tripoli despite the broader decline of the Crusader states. He was known for his diplomatic skills leadership and ability to navigate the complex political landscape of the Levant. Raymond’s efforts ensured that Tripoli remained a key player in the region contributing to its defense against advancing Islamic forces. His death was mourned by his contemporaries and he was remembered as a wise and capable ruler. The County of Tripoli continued under his successors who maintained his policies of diplomacy and fortification. Raymond’s life symbolizes the enduring struggle and resilience of the Crusader states in the face of overwhelming odds.